class.description:
A [SettingsServer] manages one [b]domain[/b] of settings, for example [code]"audio"[/code] or [code]"game"[/code]. The values of a domain are the stored properties of a [Resource] that the domain creates in [method _create_settings]. The server loads them from a file of the player, pushes them to the engine, reads the engine state back and saves the changes. [SettingsServerManager] keeps all settings servers and applies them while the game starts.
[b]Three copies.[/b] A server holds three copies of its settings resource:
- [b]defaults[/b] ([method get_default_settings]): the values a player gets without a settings file. They are built in this order: the default values of the class, then the engine state captured at startup ([method _read_from_engine] and the getters of the bindings), then an optional [code]res://[/code] resource named in the project setting [code]settings/defaults/<domain>[/code], for example [code]settings/defaults/audio = "res://audio_defaults.tres"[/code]. Only the properties that exist in both resources are taken from that resource.
- [b]applied[/b] ([method get_applied_settings]): what the engine uses now.
- [b]pending[/b] ([method get_settings]): what [method set_value] changes. [method get_dirty_setting_names] lists the values that differ from the applied copy.
[b]Apply mode.[/b] In [constant APPLY_MODE_IMMEDIATE] (the default), every [method set_value] calls [method apply] at once. In [constant APPLY_MODE_MANUAL] the changes stay pending until you call [method apply], for an options menu with an "Apply" button; [method revert] drops them. [method apply] pushes the changed values to the engine and emits [signal applied].
[b]Bindings.[/b] A setting is pushed to the engine in one of three ways: a property of an object ([method bind_setting_property]), a property of an engine singleton ([method bind_setting_singleton_property]) or two callables ([method bind_setting_callbacks]). Settings without a binding are passed to [method _apply]. The same bindings are used to read the engine state in [method read_from_engine] and for the defaults. Use [enum SettingFlags] to mark values that need a restart or a confirmation.
[b]Confirmation.[/b] [method apply_with_confirmation] applies the changes and starts a timer. If the player does not call [method confirm_changes] in time, the old values are restored, for example when a new video mode shows a black screen. The timeout is checked every frame of the [SceneTree] ([signal SceneTree.process_frame]); without a [SceneTree] call [method confirm_changes] or [method cancel_confirmation] yourself. In [constant APPLY_MODE_IMMEDIATE], values with [constant SETTING_FLAG_CONFIRM] go through the confirmation automatically. The default timeout is [member ProjectSettings.settings/apply/confirmation_timeout].
[b]Storage.[/b] Each domain has one file, [code]user://settings/<domain>.ini[/code] (plain INI that players can edit), [code].json[/code], [code].xml[/code], [code].toml[/code] or [code].yaml[/code], chosen with [member ProjectSettings.settings/storage/format] (a [enum ResourceTextSerializer.Format] value, see [method SettingsServerManager.get_storage_format]). A [ListSettingsServer] can also use [code].csv[/code]. A format that is not available (TOML or YAML with its module disabled) falls back to INI with a warning. With [member ProjectSettings.settings/storage/only_changed_values] only the values that differ from the defaults are written (INI and JSON; XML, TOML and YAML files always hold all values), so values added in a game update reach players who never changed them. The file contains the entry [code]metadata/settings_version[/code] with [method get_format_version]; if it is older than the current version, [method _migrate] converts the data when the file is loaded.
[method load] reads the file into the pending copy (call [method apply] afterwards to use it; [SettingsServerManager] does this at startup). If there is no file, it returns [constant ERR_FILE_NOT_FOUND] and the defaults stay, without a message: this is the normal first start. If the file is broken, it prints a warning and the defaults are used; the file is not overwritten until the next [method save]. [method save] writes the applied state (the confirmed state while a confirmation is pending).
With [member ProjectSettings.settings/storage/autosave], a save is queued after every [method apply] and runs at the end of the frame, at most once per frame. This is needed on the Web, where [code]user://[/code] is synchronized to IndexedDB asynchronously and the game gets no notification on exit, and on Android, where the process can be killed after the game is paused. On exit, [SettingsServerManager] runs queued saves.
[b]Session overrides.[/b] [method set_session_override] pushes a value to the engine without changing the stored value, for example from a command line argument. It is never saved. A later [method set_value] for the same setting removes the override.
[b]Writing a domain for your game.[/b] Extend [SettingsServer], implement [method _get_domain] and [method _create_settings], and list the script in the project setting [member ProjectSettings.settings/startup/custom_servers]. [SettingsServerManager] creates and starts it.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
# game_settings_resource.gd
class_name GameSettingsResource
extends Resource

@export_range(0.0, 1.0, 0.01) var music_volume := 0.8
@export_enum("Easy", "Normal", "Hard") var difficulty := 1
@export var show_hints := true

# game_settings.gd, listed in settings/startup/custom_servers.
extends SettingsServer

func _get_domain() -> StringName:
	return &"game"

func _create_settings() -> Resource:
	return GameSettingsResource.new()

func _apply(settings: Resource, names: PackedStringArray) -> void:
	if "music_volume" in names:
		var bus = AudioServer.get_bus_index("Music")
		AudioServer.set_bus_volume_db(bus, linear_to_db(settings.music_volume))

# Anywhere in the game:
func _on_difficulty_selected(index):
	var game = SettingsServerManager.get_server(&"game")
	game.set_value("difficulty", index) # Applied and saved to user://settings/game.ini.
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
// GameSettingsResource.cs
[GlobalClass]
public partial class GameSettingsResource : Resource
{
    [Export(PropertyHint.Range, "0,1,0.01")] public float MusicVolume { get; set; } = 0.8f;
    [Export(PropertyHint.Enum, "Easy,Normal,Hard")] public int Difficulty { get; set; } = 1;
    [Export] public bool ShowHints { get; set; } = true;
}

// GameSettings.cs, listed in settings/startup/custom_servers.
public partial class GameSettings : SettingsServer
{
    public override StringName _GetDomain() => "game";

    public override Resource _CreateSettings() => new GameSettingsResource();

    public override void _Apply(Resource settings, string[] names)
    {
        if (Array.IndexOf(names, "music_volume") >= 0)
        {
            int bus = AudioServer.GetBusIndex("Music");
            AudioServer.SetBusVolumeDb(bus, Mathf.LinearToDb(((GameSettingsResource)settings).MusicVolume));
        }
    }
}

// Anywhere in the game:
var game = SettingsServerManager.GetServer("game");
game.SetValue("difficulty", 2);
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
[b]Note:[/b] In C#, the settings server of a C++ domain that is an engine singleton is reached through its [code]Singleton[/code] property, for example [code]AudioDeviceSettingsServer.Singleton.Apply()[/code]. The generator only puts the methods of the class itself into the static class, so the methods of [SettingsServer] are not available as [code]AudioDeviceSettingsServer.Apply()[/code].
[b]Note:[/b] The API is for the main thread only. Signals are emitted on the main thread.
[b]Note:[/b] Settings files are stored in [code]user://[/code], which is available on all platforms, including the Web export.
